epinephrine >0.25 mcg/kg/min for >4 hours). 2) Blood product thresholds: transfuse platelets only if <10,000/mcL or active bleeding (not present); no evidence for RBC transfusion unless Hb <7 g/dL. 3) Lactate clearance goal: target lactate reduction by ≥20% within 6 hours; repeat lactate q2-4h. Disposition planning: ICU admission, step-down to ward after vasopressor wean and clinical improvement. Potential complications: acute respiratory distress syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, acute kidney injury requiring RRT, multi-organ failure, antibiotic-associated Clostridioides difficile infection. Need for vasopressors: yes, norepinephrine first-line. Assessment for ICU admission: mandatory. Prostate examination: digital rectal exam to assess for prostatitis/abscess. Imaging considerations: renal ultrasound (or CT if stable) to evaluate for obstruction, abscess, or emphysematous pyelonephritis. Source control beyond catheterization: if prostatic abscess, urology for drainage; if renal abscess, percutaneous drainage.",
